  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 400 PM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Well below average temperatures in cold air damming \nwill continue for the next couple of days and will be accompanied by \nanother wave of showers and storms with locally heavy rain that \nmoves across the area from late tonight to midday Saturday.\n\nThe cold front is currently pinned near or just inland of the \nsouthern NC coast today, which is where all the deep convection with \nlightning is currently focused. Meanwhile a CAD pattern with surface \nhigh pressure wedging in from the north is resulting in cool NE \nflow, low overcast, and just some very patchy light rain and drizzle \nacross central NC. SPC mesoanalysis depicts any CAPE is confined to \nClinton southeastward. Thus temperatures will largely stay stuck in \nthe 70s today, and the current high at FAY is only 77F, meaning the \ndaily record low maximum of 81F is certainly in jeopardy.\n\nA shortwave currently over SE MO will track ESE into the TN Valley \nand Southern Appalachians tonight, then across the Carolinas during \nthe day on Saturday. This will provide some additional forcing for \nascent and help the boundary pivot back NW again into SE parts of \nour region tonight. Thus moisture and instability will tick back up \nwith PW values increasing to 2-2.5 inches and MUCAPE of 500-1000 \nJ/kg across our southern Piedmont, Sandhills, and central/southern \nCoastal Plain. So expect an area of widespread showers and isolated \nstorms to move across the area tonight into Saturday morning, with \nthe highest rainfall rates from about 4 AM to noon. \n\nWPC does have a slight (level 2 of 4) risk for excessive rainfall \nfor this event across our SE zones. The 12z HREF depicts increasing \nneighborhood probabilities of exceeding FFG again tonight, but only \nin the 20-25% range, as the higher probabilities remain pinned near \nthe coastline where the better instability will still be. WPC QPF \nthrough 18z Saturday ranges from 0.25-0.5\" in the far northern \nPiedmont to 0.75-1\" from Raleigh south and east. While the 12z HREF \nLPMM depicts some 1-2\" bullseyes, neighborhood probabilities of \nrainfall rates exceeding 1\"/hr are much lower than they had been for \nyesterday's event, which makes sense given the marginal instability \nespecially over the northern Piedmont. Thus opted against issuing a \nFlood Watch at this time, but can't rule out some isolated urban and \nsmall stream flooding given how saturated the ground is now. Rain \nwill largely come to an end from NW to SE in the late morning and \nafternoon. Temperatures will again be quite cool for this time of \nyear with highs in the mid-70s to lower-80s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... More typical July weather returns Sunday and \npersists into next week with diurnally driven convection and highs \nin the upper 80s to lower 90s.\n\nAfter another round of showers and embedded storms rolls through the \nregion tonight into roughly midday Saturday, drier weather takes \nhold late Saturday and Sunday. The pattern also returns to a more \ntypical late July pattern, with temperatures back into the upper 80s \nto lower 90s through most of the extended. The warmest day may be \nTuesday ahead of another front in the low to middle 90s. As for \nstorm chances, they appear to be more diurnally driven as central NC \nresides in a generally northwest flow aloft. Some embedded \ndisturbances could ripple through to produce afternoon and evening \nstorms. The more favored time period in the ensemble guidance is \nlooking like the Tue-Wed period along and ahead of a cold front. SPC \ncurrently has a Day 5 slight risk for this period and recent AI \nguidance also has been honing in on a severe risk. WPC highlights a \nmarginal excessive rain risk at this time also and given the slower \npassage of frontal systems, we may be dealing with a combined \nthreat. However, there is still enough model spread to limit our \noverall confidence at this time range, but something we continue to \nkeep a close eye on.",
